Part 1. Facts survey

### 1.1. Facts given in the task
- Review title: “Cotrimoxazole prophylaxis for opportunistic infections in children with HIV infection”
- Objective: Assess effects of routinely administered cotrimoxazole on death and illness episodes in children with HIV 
infection, and in infants of HIV‐infected mothers.
- Background:
  - Most HIV-infected children live in low-income countries without ART access.
  - Prevention/early treatment of opportunistic infections is key.
  - Cotrimoxazole (trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) is cheap, broad-spectrum, effective against Pneumocystis jiroveci 
pneumonia (PCP), relatively safe.
  - Early HIV diagnosis in infants is complicated by maternal antibodies; prophylaxis based on maternal status is one 
approach.
  - Routine prophylaxis delivery is difficult in low-resource settings and may increase resistance.
- Selection criteria:
  - Randomised or quasi-randomised trials.
  - Intervention: routinely administered cotrimoxazole.
  - Comparator: placebo or no treatment.
  - Population: children <15 years with HIV infection, or children <18 months with HIV-infected mothers.
- Provided studies (titles, abstracts, indices):
  - Study 1 (Zambia, CHAP): Double-blind RCT, children 1–14 years with clinical HIV, daily cotrimoxazole vs placebo; 
primary outcomes mortality and adverse events. N=541 randomized; 7 later HIV-negative excluded. Median follow-up 19 
months. Deaths: 74 (28%) CTX vs 112 (42%) placebo; HR 0.57 (95% CI 0.43–0.77), p=0.0002. Benefit consistent >12 months 
and across ages and CD4 strata. Grade 3/4 AEs: 16 (6%) CTX vs 18 (7%) placebo. Severe neutropenia on one occasion: 16 
(6%) CTX vs 7 (3%) placebo (p=0.06). PCP identified in 1/73 NPAs from children with pneumonia (placebo group). Trial 
stopped early on DSMC recommendation (Oct 2003).
  - Study 2 (Abidjan adults, early prophylaxis): Adult RCT; not a pediatric study.
  - Study 3 (AIDS Kaposi’s sarcoma): Adult randomized trial; not pediatric; no routine prophylaxis comparator aligned 
with inclusion.
  - Study 4 (case series, homosexual men): Adult case series; not RCT; not pediatric.
  - Study 5 (Zimbabwe pediatric autopsy): Observational; not randomized prophylaxis; includes HIV-seropositive children,
focuses on pulmonary findings.
  - Study 6 (Rwanda pediatric cohort): Prospective cohort of HIV-exposed/infected vs uninfected children; not randomized
prophylaxis.
  - Study 7 (Malawi pediatric cohort): Prospective morbidity study among HIV-infected/uninfected; not randomized 
prophylaxis.
  - Study 8 (Abidjan TB adults): Adult RCT in HIV-positive TB patients; not pediatric.
  - Study 9 (Review on MTCT): Not an RCT; not pediatric prophylaxis trial.
  - Study 10 (South African pediatric PCP incidence): Prospective diagnostic/epidemiologic; not randomized prophylaxis 
(although notes prophylaxis status vs PCP incidence).

### 1.2. Facts to look up
